WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:04.200
I'll actually just quickly show some of this stuff.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.199
Okay, so we got a little actor here with a widget component on it.

3
00:00:09.199 --> 00:00:15.199
So if I just quickly jump into this, you can see this is just an actor I've made with a widget component on it.

4
00:00:15.199 --> 00:00:20.199
As you might assume, because this widget can exist in space, this is a scene component,

5
00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:22.199
therefore it's part of the scene component hierarchy.

6
00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:24.199
Therefore, all the things that apply to it would apply here.

7
00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:30.199
So their position is described relatively to the thing that they're attached to, etc, etc.

8
00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:32.740
You've got all these various properties.

9
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.740
You can specify a material that's used for this widget.

10
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.740
Like we said, this is our world or screen space.

11
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.740
This is the widget that we're actually using, our draw size.

12
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:46.740
This is actually quite an important element, particularly when you're in world space,

13
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.740
because this kind of means two things at once.

14
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.740
One is the actual canvas that is used to draw this thing.

15
00:00:54.740 --> 00:01:07.620
So if I were to create some kind of example widget, this were just a border,

16
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:14.480
and we're going to make it some horribly garish color, like vibrant magenta,

17
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.480
and then put an image in the top left.

18
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:27.799
And that's just going to be just going to give it some kind of icon, this thing.

19
00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:31.799
And then I want to specify that as our 3D widget.

20
00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:34.799
You can really see how this would kind of come in.

21
00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:37.799
So I'm just going to do new widget blueprint.

22
00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:44.099
You can see that our widget actually being displayed.

23
00:01:44.099 --> 00:01:54.099
But if I were to adjust this such that our draw size were more like 1000 by 500,

24
00:01:54.099 --> 00:01:58.099
you can see our actual icon.

25
00:01:58.099 --> 00:02:00.099
It's not like our widget has scaled up.

26
00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:05.099
It's just the kind of window it's being drawn into is larger.

27
00:02:05.099 --> 00:02:10.099
So it's just kind of worth bearing that in mind and understanding what kind of consequences that is going to have.

28
00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:12.099
It doesn't always mean the content is going to get bigger.

29
00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:16.099
It just means that the space that the content is getting drawn into is bigger.

30
00:02:16.099 --> 00:02:23.099
So it kind of very much depends on how you've set your widget up for whether or not that is going to work.

31
00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:30.650
If I go back to 3D widget, which is what this is actually set up for, and I change that back to 300 by 200.

32
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.710
Like I said, this means kind of two things.

33
00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:34.710
So one is the kind of space that you have.

34
00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:39.710
The other thing is that draw size maps directly to how big it is in world, as you've kind of already seen.

35
00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:43.710
So the standard implementation is each pixel here is one unreal unit.

36
00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:49.710
And therefore a 300 by 200 widget is going to be three metres wide, two metres tall.

37
00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:55.710
If you don't want that to be the relationship, if you don't want it to be one unit is one centimetre,

38
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:59.710
the only way I'm aware of changing that is by changing the scale of the component.

39
00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:04.710
I don't normally advise scaling components, avoiding it whenever you can.

40
00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:10.710
But I'm not aware of another way to change the kind of ratio of draw size to actual size in world

41
00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.710
other than to change the scale of the component.

42
00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:15.969
But there may be other ways that I'm not aware of.

43
00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:22.969
Like I said, we've got our pivot here, so this is saying the centre of our component is going to be the centre of the widget.

44
00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:26.379
So if I kind of just briefly demonstrate that.

45
00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:28.379
OK, well, that's not actually showing my way.

46
00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:38.919
Oh, no, probably is actually. If I change this to 300 by 100,

47
00:03:38.919 --> 00:03:41.620
that probably actually is where the component is.

48
00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:46.620
Yeah, it's just that this widget is again not actually taking up that much space.

49
00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.620
So it's kind of just getting pushed into the top left.

50
00:03:50.620 --> 00:04:04.990
So if I were to change this to be more like 200 by 50, let's say.

51
00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:05.990
Yeah, that's a little bit more like it.

52
00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:10.990
So you can see our pivot is pretty much over the centre of the thing.

53
00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:15.990
That's the only reason it's not is just because I haven't actually accurately matched the size of the widget itself.

54
00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:24.990
But if I were to change our pivot to be 0.51, you could see it's not like less is being drawn, but it's just that the geometry is kind of being pushed up.

55
00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.990
Where is the kind of jump that the box that represents our widget would be kind of more like here.

56
00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:33.990
Whereas when the pivot is 0.5, 0.5, it would be drawn more like here.

57
00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:38.990
And you can kind of guess what a pivot of zero is going to do.

58
00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:40.990
It's going to offset it to the left.

59
00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:44.990
So the left side starts where the component position is.

60
00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:46.990
So that's the effect of the pivot.

61
00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.589
So you can see the obvious benefits here.

62
00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:54.589
There are plenty of games that would utilise this kind of 3D widget drawing functionality.

63
00:04:54.589 --> 00:05:11.589
We've seen things like Dead Space with this kind of diegetic UIs, something like Splinter Style Conviction for apparently all my game references are really old for some kind of spatial UIs and stuff like that.

64
00:05:11.589 --> 00:05:15.589
You can imagine lots and lots of cool functionalities here.

65
00:05:15.589 --> 00:05:23.589
But the kind of other side of this coin is that once a widget is actually 3D in world, maybe you want the capability to actually interact with it.

66
00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:27.589
Maybe we want to go like full diegetic and these are actual screens in world that you can interact with.

67
00:05:27.589 --> 00:05:30.589
And that's where the widget interaction component comes in.

68
00:05:30.589 --> 00:05:35.589
This essentially gives us the functionality for a player to interact with 3D widgets in world.

69
00:05:35.589 --> 00:05:43.589
Like I already said, it sits on the player portal character and it's driven by traces and this component can actually simulate key presses.

70
00:05:44.589 --> 00:05:47.589
Let me just actually just demonstrate this quickly.

71
00:05:47.589 --> 00:05:48.589
So we've got this.

72
00:05:48.589 --> 00:05:50.589
That's actually what this demo scene is for.

73
00:05:50.589 --> 00:05:53.589
We've got this widget trace of Pawn.

74
00:05:53.589 --> 00:05:56.589
If I just quickly jump inside that, you can see how this is set up.

75
00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:58.980
We've just got a standard.

76
00:05:58.980 --> 00:05:59.980
This just inherits from character.

77
00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:08.980
So it's got all the standard character stuff, but it's just got this additional widget interactive component on it, which has a couple of different things to be worth bearing in mind.

78
00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:16.980
First off, we have a virtual user index, which is essentially which user should we pretend this widget is interacting on behalf of.

79
00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:28.980
Essentially, the widget interact component pretends that they were like a UI user and it just turns our 3D scans into kind of like pretending there's like a mouse cursor or something like that over this widget geometry.

80
00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.980
So which user should it pretend to be?

81
00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:36.980
Pointer index is relevant if you're doing kind of like touch based inputs where you might have like, you know, multiple fingers.

82
00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:39.980
So the pointer index is which kind of finger should it represent.

83
00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:43.980
By standard, if you're just doing kind of mouse things, it's going to remain at zero.

84
00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:50.360
We have the trace channel, which is essentially because this is all being done off all of this logic works off traces.

85
00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:54.360
Which channel should we be using the trace against this?

86
00:06:54.360 --> 00:07:02.360
The thing that this needs to pair up with is you need to make sure this is a channel that the 3D widget you're going to be interacting with actually blocks.

87
00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:10.360
If we go to our 3D widget, the standard collision setup for any 3D widget is a collision preset of UI.

88
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:15.360
And if we look at what that preset looks like, it overlaps every channel apart from visibility, which it blocks.

89
00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:25.360
Obviously, if that's not completely suitable for your game's needs, you could create like a UI trace channel and have your widget interaction trace on that and have your UI block that.

90
00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:28.360
But visibility is fine for this situation.

91
00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.360
Interaction distant, pretty self-explanatory, so it'll only trace this far.

92
00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.360
Any widget that's further than that, it won't detect.

93
00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:43.360
And then we've got this quite interesting setting here, which is essentially what's the logic that we use for where to draw this trace from?

94
00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:55.360
So all of these settings are pretty self-explanatory, but world will trace from the location of our component in the direction that our component is facing, regardless of where we put that component.

95
00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.360
So we could detach it from our player, we could fly around the scene, it's just wherever that component is.

96
00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:08.360
Mouse, we'll interpret the mouse location of our first local player, because really there can only be one mouse anyway.

97
00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:14.459
And turn that into screen space and draw a line from that, into world space, sorry.

98
00:08:14.459 --> 00:08:18.459
Take that screen space position, turn it into world space and draw a line from that.

99
00:08:18.459 --> 00:08:24.459
Center of screen is kind of the same idea, just the center of the screen of our first local player controller.

100
00:08:24.459 --> 00:08:33.460
And then we have custom where we can essentially provide a custom function that will specify if we need gameplay to drive this in some way.

101
00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:42.460
But most of the time when you're trying to do that, you could just be setting the world location of the component, and that's probably going to be a little bit more clear what you're actually trying to do.

102
00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:45.220
So if we just go for center of screen.

103
00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:53.220
The other thing to mention is that you've got this show debug functionality, which is a very easy and helpful way to demonstrate when this is actually working.

104
00:08:53.220 --> 00:09:00.990
So if we go here, you can see when I'm in range of the widget, my cursor starts appearing.

105
00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:08.990
You see it appears a little bit above and below, and that's because our widget geometry is actually slightly larger than the widget itself.

106
00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:14.990
This is this whole, you know, the fact that it's 50 pixels tall, even though this thing is probably only 30 pixels tall or something.

107
00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:17.990
And if I left click, it actually appears as a window.

108
00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:21.990
Now, all the options aren't displaying because now my window isn't big enough.

109
00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:27.990
So if I just quickly go back and change that to however many pixels tall it was before, I think it was like 200.

110
00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:41.809
Now, if I do that again, you can see all this extra space at the bottom because this is technically the plane is here.

111
00:09:41.809 --> 00:09:44.809
But this particular bit of the widget just isn't over it.

112
00:09:44.809 --> 00:09:47.809
But now when I click, I get all my options and I can interact with these.

113
00:09:47.809 --> 00:09:49.809
And this works literally like a normal UI.

114
00:09:49.809 --> 00:09:54.809
This could work as a UI button and I'm interacting with it exactly like that.

115
00:09:54.809 --> 00:10:00.809
So the only other kind of piece of this puzzle, we've got all this functionality set up for where the widget is, what trace channel it's drawing on.

116
00:10:00.809 --> 00:10:09.809
The only other thing is to make our widget actually try and interact when we left click, which is done in our event graph of our player controller.

117
00:10:09.809 --> 00:10:13.809
You can see we've just got a little bit of setup for movement and turning.

118
00:10:13.809 --> 00:10:21.809
And the key component here is that on our left mouse button, on the widget interaction component itself, we call this press pointer key.

119
00:10:21.809 --> 00:10:24.809
And on let go, we do release pointer key.

120
00:10:24.809 --> 00:10:31.809
And it's this that is kind of funneling this input down through our widget interaction component as though it were coming through our mouse.

121
00:10:31.809 --> 00:10:38.809
You can also actually funnel keyboard keys through press pointer key, so it's not exclusively limited to keyboard keys.

122
00:10:38.809 --> 00:10:41.809
But yeah, that's just worth mentioning.

123
00:10:41.809 --> 00:10:45.809
In previous games, I've actually used this to do like interactive PC screens.

124
00:10:46.809 --> 00:10:53.809
You can imagine a whole bunch of implementations for this that aren't just clicking on a button hovering in the world.

125
00:10:53.809 --> 00:10:58.379
So yeah, very powerful and you can have a lot of fun with that.

126
00:10:58.379 --> 00:11:03.639
So now we're kind of at the end of the kind of more formal structure bit of the talk.

127
00:11:03.639 --> 00:11:07.639
And there's just a couple of other things I just wanted to mention before we finally get to the end.

128
00:11:07.639 --> 00:11:10.639
Obviously, UMG is a huge topic here.

129
00:11:11.639 --> 00:11:17.639
But there's just a couple of things that if you're starting out in UMG, there's a couple of things to kind of be aware of and look out for.

130
00:11:17.639 --> 00:11:24.639
This isn't really the talk to kind of go into detail about each of these things.

131
00:11:24.639 --> 00:11:30.639
But just if you're starting to utilize UMG, there's a couple of little things that you can hit up against that can get really confusing.

132
00:11:30.639 --> 00:11:33.639
So I just thought were worth very, very briefly mentioning.

133
00:11:33.639 --> 00:11:36.639
Hence the kind of huge slightly distorted list.

134
00:11:36.639 --> 00:11:38.639
So first off, DPI scaling.

135
00:11:38.639 --> 00:11:46.639
One of the things when you're putting UIs onto screens, you might encounter situations where your UI seems to think it's one size or it's drawing at one size.

136
00:11:46.639 --> 00:11:48.639
It doesn't necessarily make sense.

137
00:11:48.639 --> 00:11:50.639
I've got a little demo scene that demonstrates this.

138
00:11:50.639 --> 00:11:55.639
Again, this project is downloadable, so you can kind of jump in here and see everything I'm doing.

139
00:11:55.639 --> 00:12:06.639
But if I go to a map and go to a DPI scale example, you can see as I play, I'm putting a widget onto my screen and I'm printing the different sizes of it.

140
00:12:06.639 --> 00:12:12.639
We've got the local size, so the widget, the size of the widget itself thinks it is.

141
00:12:12.639 --> 00:12:17.639
So it thinks it's 1880 by 1080, which is like not crazy far off.

142
00:12:17.639 --> 00:12:19.639
But this is, you know, I'm on a 1080 monitor.

143
00:12:19.639 --> 00:12:23.639
This screen is clearly not 1080 pixels high, right?

144
00:12:23.639 --> 00:12:25.639
This whole monitor is 1080 pixels high.

145
00:12:25.639 --> 00:12:28.639
So that's clearly a little bit off and weird.

146
00:12:28.639 --> 00:12:31.639
The absolute size is a little bit closer, probably.

147
00:12:31.639 --> 00:12:36.639
But this would get a little bit weirder if you've got a multi monitor set up and it's in a different location.

148
00:12:36.639 --> 00:12:40.639
Or maybe you've got a window and it's kind of like, you know, off to the side here.

149
00:12:40.639 --> 00:12:42.639
It's going to be describing something a little bit different.

150
00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:46.740
A kind of viewport size.

151
00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:51.740
So this is, again, kind of close, but not necessarily completely right.

152
00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:54.740
And then the desired size of our widget.

153
00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:57.740
And then we've got this other value, which is our scale.

154
00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:04.740
So if I just kind of start changing this a little bit, you see these numbers changing.

155
00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:11.740
And in an extreme case, you can see that, like, clearly the size of my widget is like something's going wrong here, right?

156
00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:18.740
Because it thinks it's 2300 pixels wide, which is like my monitor literally cannot display that resolution.

157
00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:26.740
And the reason for that and the reason that you may be getting kind of weird sizing issues where your UIs don't seem to be at the size that they think they are

158
00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:29.740
is down to this thing, this concept called DPI scaling.

159
00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:32.740
Essentially, DPI scaling is something, it's not a bug.

160
00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:37.740
Its intended functionality is to make sure that your UIs, you author them at one size,

161
00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:41.740
but obviously they need to be able to work on screens of lots of different sizes.

162
00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:45.740
You know, the standard is 1920 by 1080, but some people only have access to 720 screens.

163
00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:49.740
Some people are still on 1024 by 768 screens.

164
00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:51.740
Some people are on 4K.

165
00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:55.740
So you don't want your UI to only work on the one resolution that you authored it for.

166
00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:58.740
So that's where DPI scaling comes in.

167
00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:04.740
What DPI scaling actually does is it reads the shortest side of your screen

168
00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:13.740
and essentially takes that and puts it into a function to establish how much should we scale this screen by.

169
00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:19.740
So when I say the shortest side, you can see that is either width or height, whichever is shorter.

170
00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:25.740
And you can see this happening because as I get square, eventually when it stops being square

171
00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:30.740
and it starts being thinner rather than tall, you'll see my scale changing.

172
00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:35.740
And now that it's thinner than tall, my scale is now going down.

173
00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:40.740
And it's at the point that I reach the point of equilibrium.

174
00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:42.740
It now stays static.

175
00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:45.740
So I do mean it when I say the shortest side.

176
00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:48.740
It's at runtime, whichever of the two sides is shorter.

177
00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:50.740
And this is in the project settings.

178
00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:58.220
If you just look for DPI scale, there is literally an engine user interface.

179
00:14:58.220 --> 00:15:05.419
There's literally a graph here that says what's the standard application scale,

180
00:15:05.419 --> 00:15:08.419
what's the rule it should use, by default it's the shortest side.

181
00:15:08.419 --> 00:15:12.419
And essentially this is just, as you can see, a pretty linear curve.

182
00:15:12.419 --> 00:15:15.419
You can see it's got keys at the kind of standard resolutions.

183
00:15:15.419 --> 00:15:17.419
So 1080, 720.

184
00:15:17.419 --> 00:15:20.419
Again, we're keying the shortest side here.

185
00:15:20.419 --> 00:15:24.419
So our 1920 and 1080, which of those two numbers is smaller.

186
00:15:24.419 --> 00:15:26.419
And what scale should that map to?

187
00:15:26.419 --> 00:15:29.419
So when your screen is 1080, the scale is 1.

188
00:15:29.419 --> 00:15:32.419
When your shortest side is 720, the scale is 0.6.

189
00:15:32.419 --> 00:15:34.419
And all the things in between.

190
00:15:34.419 --> 00:15:38.419
And you may have noticed that the scale stopped at 0.44444.

191
00:15:38.419 --> 00:15:41.419
And that's because there are no more keys on this curve.

192
00:15:41.419 --> 00:15:44.419
But yeah, it's just worth noting that DPI scale is a thing.

193
00:15:44.419 --> 00:15:48.419
Here is where you can tweak it if you ever need to do that and just be aware of it.

194
00:15:50.509 --> 00:15:52.509
The widget reflector we've kind of already talked about.

195
00:15:52.509 --> 00:15:56.509
This is basically a very, very powerful debugging tool

196
00:15:56.509 --> 00:16:00.509
that gives you the ability to debug UIs at runtime.

197
00:16:00.509 --> 00:16:02.509
You can do this in...

198
00:16:02.509 --> 00:16:10.940
Let's just go to a more complicated example.

199
00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:12.940
So if I open up my widget reflector,

200
00:16:12.940 --> 00:16:14.940
I can do that through a console command,

201
00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:17.940
or you can do it through your UMG window as we saw.

202
00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:19.940
There's a whole bunch of things,

203
00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:21.940
but the key thing that I tend to use a lot

204
00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:24.940
is you've got this option here where you can pick a widget on your screen.

205
00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:26.940
So you can either pick a hit testable widget,

206
00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:28.940
so this widget is kind of visible,

207
00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:30.940
or you can pick a painted widget,

208
00:16:30.940 --> 00:16:32.940
so one that you can just see.

209
00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:34.940
And you can hover over anything

210
00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:38.940
and you can see all this information updating in the middle of my box.

211
00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:40.940
And then whenever I want to, I can hit escape

212
00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:42.940
and it stops trying to read whichever widget I'm over

213
00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:44.940
and kind of just freezes on the last one I had selected.

214
00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:46.940
And then you can look into your details panel,

215
00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:48.940
all the information about that.

216
00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:49.940
You can see the full hierarchy.

217
00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:51.940
Obviously, this is absolutely gigantic,

218
00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:53.940
so you might need to drag this over

219
00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:56.940
to get a more full picture of your hierarchy.

220
00:16:56.940 --> 00:16:58.940
Some other bits of information that are quite useful here

221
00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:00.940
is you can also filter this.

222
00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:02.940
So if you go to filter,

223
00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:06.940
because this is going all the way up to your Unreal root screen,

224
00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:09.940
you can actually see all the different bits of it getting focused.

225
00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:12.940
So here's the point where it's actually inside my Unreal editor.

226
00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:16.940
That's the border up here.

227
00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:18.940
You can see this is our game overlay.

228
00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:20.940
Then we're inside the level editor.

229
00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:23.940
And all of this stuff says what classes is it?

230
00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:25.940
What visibility state is it in?

231
00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.940
So if you have widgets that don't seem to be appearing,

232
00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:30.940
you could use the widget editor to see,

233
00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:32.940
are they tinted the wrong color?

234
00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:34.940
Are they there, but they're kind of off screen?

235
00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:35.940
What's going on?

236
00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:37.940
Are they even added to this hierarchy?

237
00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:39.940
This is obviously, all of this hierarchy here

238
00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:41.940
is just the visible hierarchy.

239
00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:45.940
So anything that isn't actually added to the screen,

240
00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:47.940
you won't be able to debug through this.

241
00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:51.940
But you can even use this to look at things actually in the editor itself

242
00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:55.940
to be like, oh, I really, I wonder how they did that widget setup.

243
00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:58.940
And you can use this to kind of focus on these things

244
00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:00.940
and kind of see how it's being constructed.

245
00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:03.940
So it's very, very useful kind of not just debugging tool,

246
00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:07.940
but also kind of like archeology tool, I suppose.

247
00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:09.940
The other kind of useful thing is, like I said,

248
00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:11.940
you can filter all of this down to be like,

249
00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:13.940
I don't care about any of the editor functionality.

250
00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:15.940
I just want to start at the point that UMG is.

251
00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:18.940
And that gives us the ability to kind of really trim down

252
00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:20.940
the number of widgets we're talking about.

253
00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:22.940
And also, if you still have a very complex hierarchy,

254
00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:25.940
you can be like, oh, I don't care about any of this stuff.

255
00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:28.940
I want to filter down to this specific node

256
00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:30.940
and only worry about what's within this.

257
00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:36.579
And as I said, you can go through individual widgets

258
00:18:36.579 --> 00:18:39.579
and kind of like debug them, look at their various details

259
00:18:39.579 --> 00:18:42.579
and also see which widget is highlighted

260
00:18:42.579 --> 00:18:44.579
whenever you're talking about these various things.

261
00:18:44.579 --> 00:18:47.579
This is the vertical box and this is the geometry it has.

262
00:18:47.579 --> 00:18:49.579
It's a little bit difficult to see

263
00:18:49.579 --> 00:18:50.579
because these things are currently white.

264
00:18:50.579 --> 00:18:52.579
But you can see as I'm hovering over the text box,

265
00:18:52.579 --> 00:18:53.579
we get the block around there.

266
00:18:53.579 --> 00:18:55.579
So, yeah, widget reflector,

267
00:18:55.579 --> 00:18:57.579
very, very powerful debugging tool.

268
00:18:57.579 --> 00:18:59.579
Be aware that it's there, use it liberally.

269
00:18:59.579 --> 00:19:02.470
Gamepad navigation and focus.

270
00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:03.470
This is quite a deep topic.

271
00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:07.470
But all I'm going to say is that there is a kind of set,

272
00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:09.470
a map in this example that just shows you

273
00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:12.470
how you might handle gamepad input

274
00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:14.470
because this is a kind of standard thing

275
00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:17.470
that's a little bit underserved in Unreal documentation.

276
00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:19.470
So there's a little example level set up

277
00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:22.470
for how you can handle gamepad focus.

278
00:19:22.470 --> 00:19:24.470
We essentially put a widget on screen.

279
00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:27.470
The key thing is basically just focusing a widget.

280
00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:29.470
And at that point, a lot of the behaviour

281
00:19:29.470 --> 00:19:32.470
will kind of just work.

282
00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:35.470
You just kind of need to know how navigation works in Unreal.

283
00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:37.470
So I'm just going to actually connect up my gamepad.

284
00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:39.470
Just give me one second.

285
00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:44.549
So if I play, the kind of key things to bear in mind

286
00:19:44.549 --> 00:19:46.549
is that you set focus.

287
00:19:46.549 --> 00:19:49.549
And if I just stop play and start again.

288
00:19:49.549 --> 00:19:53.809
But you also bear in mind that certain widgets

289
00:19:53.809 --> 00:19:55.809
have a kind of navigation logic to them,

290
00:19:55.809 --> 00:19:57.809
like a vertical box or a scroll box

291
00:19:57.809 --> 00:20:00.809
will look for the next entry within them.

292
00:20:00.809 --> 00:20:03.809
But the default navigation behaviour,

293
00:20:03.809 --> 00:20:05.809
I'm just using my left stick on my gamepad here.

294
00:20:05.809 --> 00:20:07.809
I haven't done any special custom setup to facilitate this.

295
00:20:07.809 --> 00:20:09.809
The default logic basically does

296
00:20:09.809 --> 00:20:11.809
what is a little bit like ray tracing.

297
00:20:11.809 --> 00:20:13.809
So the way you navigate between widgets

298
00:20:13.809 --> 00:20:16.809
using gamepad by default

299
00:20:16.809 --> 00:20:19.000
is that they just need to overlap in some way.

300
00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:23.000
So if, for example, I go from this widget

301
00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:24.000
and I try and navigate right,

302
00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.000
you'll see it skips over that widget in the middle

303
00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:27.000
and finds the next widget

304
00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:29.000
that kind of literally overlaps with it.

305
00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:31.000
So it'll jump all the way there.

306
00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:32.000
If there were nothing overlapping with it,

307
00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.000
it would not navigate at all.

308
00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:35.000
So it just wouldn't work.

309
00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:37.000
So just be aware that your sizes

310
00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:40.000
and your overlapping is actually quite relevant here.

311
00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:48.000
And also, you can interject bits of widgetry

312
00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:49.000
that aren't even focusable

313
00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:51.000
and it doesn't necessarily interrupt the flow.

314
00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:52.000
So the fact that this text block

315
00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.000
is in the middle between these two buttons

316
00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.000
doesn't necessarily matter.

317
00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:57.259
But the things that do matter,

318
00:20:57.259 --> 00:20:58.259
the only things that you can navigate to

319
00:20:58.259 --> 00:21:01.259
are things that are, A, focusable,

320
00:21:01.259 --> 00:21:03.259
because things to receive gamepad focus

321
00:21:03.259 --> 00:21:05.259
need to be focusable.

322
00:21:05.259 --> 00:21:07.259
And the other thing they need to be is visible,

323
00:21:07.259 --> 00:21:09.259
i.e. hit testable.

324
00:21:09.259 --> 00:21:12.259
We talked about those various settings available before.

325
00:21:12.259 --> 00:21:14.259
Visible is the only one that basically says

326
00:21:14.259 --> 00:21:16.259
this can be clicked on by the mouse

327
00:21:16.259 --> 00:21:19.259
or can be found by gamepad

328
00:21:19.259 --> 00:21:21.259
by kind of navigation like this.

329
00:21:21.259 --> 00:21:23.259
So you can kind of see this proven.

330
00:21:23.259 --> 00:21:26.259
We've got this couple of buttons here.

331
00:21:26.259 --> 00:21:31.480
The blue buttons are visible but not focusable.

332
00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:35.480
The green buttons are visible and focusable.

333
00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:38.480
And the red buttons are focusable but not visible.

334
00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:41.480
So no combination of visible and not focusable

335
00:21:41.480 --> 00:21:43.480
or focusable and not visible works.

336
00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:45.480
It has to be both.

337
00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:47.480
But the important thing is here,

338
00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:49.480
if I go to actually this button,

339
00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:59.680
you can see that the kind of visible

340
00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:00.680
and focusable bit of this

341
00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:02.680
is actually deep into the hierarchy.

342
00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:06.680
So the parent, this actual widget itself,

343
00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:09.259
isn't focusable.

344
00:22:09.259 --> 00:22:11.259
The widget itself isn't visible.

345
00:22:11.259 --> 00:22:13.259
But this button within it is.

346
00:22:13.259 --> 00:22:16.259
The navigation doesn't care where this widget lies.

347
00:22:16.259 --> 00:22:19.259
It just, as in it cares geometrically where it lies,

348
00:22:19.259 --> 00:22:21.259
but it doesn't care about where it lies in the hierarchy.

349
00:22:21.259 --> 00:22:25.259
It will just find a widget that is focusable and visible.

350
00:22:25.259 --> 00:22:27.259
So when the navigation occurs,

351
00:22:27.259 --> 00:22:31.259
it will navigate to this explicit widget within here.

352
00:22:31.259 --> 00:22:33.259
This widget is not the one being focused.

353
00:22:33.259 --> 00:22:35.259
This is the one being focused.

354
00:22:35.259 --> 00:22:38.259
There are events that you can bind in the tree

355
00:22:38.259 --> 00:22:40.259
to say, you know, added to focus path.

356
00:22:40.259 --> 00:22:42.259
So you can see that being used here.

357
00:22:42.259 --> 00:22:45.259
That's basically to say, I'm not the one being focused,

358
00:22:45.259 --> 00:22:47.259
but something beneath me is being focused.

359
00:22:47.259 --> 00:22:49.259
One of my children is being focused.

360
00:22:49.259 --> 00:22:51.259
And you can use that to kind of do visual feedback.

361
00:22:51.259 --> 00:22:52.259
So you can see I'm doing that here

362
00:22:52.259 --> 00:22:54.259
to kind of highlight this border in pink

363
00:22:54.259 --> 00:22:57.259
when this button has been focused.

364
00:22:57.259 --> 00:23:01.769
So that's just a very, very quick intro to gamepad focus.

365
00:23:01.769 --> 00:23:05.250
Another two things that are kind of, again, underserved

366
00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:08.250
in terms of explanations is the idea of this,

367
00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:10.250
the viewport client and the slate application file.

368
00:23:10.250 --> 00:23:12.250
Just be aware that these are classes that exist

369
00:23:12.250 --> 00:23:16.250
The game viewport client is essentially the entry point

370
00:23:16.250 --> 00:23:19.250
for any input going into the game.

371
00:23:19.250 --> 00:23:26.250
The first entry point for all input into Unreal at all

372
00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:29.250
is through the slate application.

373
00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:30.250
I think I talked about this

374
00:23:30.250 --> 00:23:32.250
in maybe the second session very briefly.

375
00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:35.250
But basically, whenever we're dealing with input,

376
00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:38.500
input goes first into slate application.

377
00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:40.500
And the first thing it will try and do

378
00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:42.500
is funnel it into your UI.

379
00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:45.500
And again, it will use that same flow

380
00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:47.500
that I talked about with our input focus

381
00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:50.500
where it will go down to the widget that you have focused.

382
00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:53.500
So in the case that we had here,

383
00:23:53.500 --> 00:23:55.500
if I press the A button,

384
00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:58.500
it will go to the button I literally have focused.

385
00:23:58.500 --> 00:23:59.500
And as it happens,

386
00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:03.500
that button is actually handling my gamepad A button.

387
00:24:03.500 --> 00:24:05.500
And so you can see it kind of flickering.

388
00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:07.500
That's because it's reacting to me pressing the button.

389
00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:09.500
But if it weren't handling that,

390
00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:11.500
naturally, that input would,

391
00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:13.500
if we look at the hierarchy,

392
00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:15.500
the input would bubble up to this border

393
00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:17.500
to see if it can do anything with that button.

394
00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:18.500
It wouldn't.

395
00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:19.500
It would go up to here

396
00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:21.500
to see if it does anything with it.

397
00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:26.500
And in the context of being inside this whole widget hierarchy,

398
00:24:26.500 --> 00:24:31.759
wherever that setup is,

399
00:24:31.759 --> 00:24:32.759
focus navigation,

400
00:24:32.759 --> 00:24:35.759
in the context of this button or whatever it was,

401
00:24:35.759 --> 00:24:36.759
it would go up to here,

402
00:24:36.759 --> 00:24:38.759
and then here, and then here,

403
00:24:38.759 --> 00:24:40.759
all the way up to your game viewport,

404
00:24:40.759 --> 00:24:46.759
and eventually it gets up to your viewport itself.

405
00:24:46.759 --> 00:24:49.759
And it is only at that point

406
00:24:49.759 --> 00:24:52.759
that it gets to your viewport,

407
00:24:52.759 --> 00:24:59.759
if the rest of your in-game UI hasn't handled that input,

408
00:24:59.759 --> 00:25:02.890
if the UI is what has focus this is,

409
00:25:02.890 --> 00:25:03.890
then it will get to your viewport,

410
00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:05.890
and the point that it goes to your viewport,

411
00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:08.890
then it will, if you're in an input mode

412
00:25:08.890 --> 00:25:13.890
that is either game only or game and UI,

413
00:25:13.890 --> 00:25:15.890
then that input will then get focused

414
00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:17.890
into your game viewport client,

415
00:25:17.890 --> 00:25:19.890
and it will then go down into your game,

416
00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:21.890
and that's where you then get input

417
00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:24.890
for movement controls, turning your camera,

418
00:25:24.890 --> 00:25:26.890
whatever standard game input you like.

419
00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:28.890
So input goes first to UI,

420
00:25:28.890 --> 00:25:31.890
and then goes to game if you're in an input mode.

421
00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:33.890
And that brings us to our various input modes,

422
00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:35.890
which I'm very briefly going to show off.

423
00:25:35.890 --> 00:25:37.890
So there are three input modes you can be in.

424
00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.890
These are functions that you call on the player controller.

425
00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:43.890
Set input mode.

426
00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:57.019
So these are three input modes you can be in.

427
00:25:57.019 --> 00:26:00.019
The standard is game and UI.

428
00:26:00.019 --> 00:26:03.019
Then we also have game only and UI only.

429
00:26:03.019 --> 00:26:07.019
UI only means you set focus to a particular widget,

430
00:26:07.019 --> 00:26:10.019
and if input gets to your viewport,

431
00:26:10.019 --> 00:26:12.019
it doesn't go to your game viewport client.

432
00:26:12.019 --> 00:26:13.019
It never goes to your game.

433
00:26:13.019 --> 00:26:16.019
It basically just turns that functionality off.

434
00:26:16.019 --> 00:26:20.089
Game only means that we focus your viewport.

435
00:26:20.089 --> 00:26:23.089
We're not expecting any input to go to any other UI.

436
00:26:23.089 --> 00:26:25.089
It goes straight to your viewport,

437
00:26:25.089 --> 00:26:28.089
and then it will funnel through to your game viewport client.

438
00:26:28.089 --> 00:26:30.089
And game and UI, which is the standard,

439
00:26:30.089 --> 00:26:32.089
means that we go to UI first,

440
00:26:32.089 --> 00:26:34.089
let UI try and handle it.

441
00:26:34.089 --> 00:26:36.089
If UI hasn't handled it,

442
00:26:36.089 --> 00:26:38.089
it eventually bubbles up to the viewport,

443
00:26:38.089 --> 00:26:40.089
and then funnels that input to your game viewport.

444
00:26:40.089 --> 00:26:44.089
So the two kind of real key classes in all of this flow

445
00:26:44.089 --> 00:26:47.089
are, again, your slate application file,

446
00:26:47.089 --> 00:26:49.089
where all of this input starts,

447
00:26:49.089 --> 00:26:51.089
and your game viewport client is the point at which

448
00:26:51.089 --> 00:26:54.089
input has gone into your game.

449
00:26:54.089 --> 00:26:56.089
It's first been checked against UI,

450
00:26:56.089 --> 00:26:58.089
and just depending on your input,

451
00:26:58.089 --> 00:27:00.089
depending on whether or not any of the UI handles that input,

452
00:27:00.089 --> 00:27:03.089
it will then eventually bubble through to your viewport

453
00:27:03.089 --> 00:27:05.089
and then make it to your viewport client

454
00:27:05.089 --> 00:27:08.089
if you're not in input mode UI only.

455
00:27:08.089 --> 00:27:12.859
Cached geometry is something that's just kind of worth knowing about.

456
00:27:12.859 --> 00:27:14.859
This is basically just in UMG,

457
00:27:14.859 --> 00:27:16.859
whenever you're in the UMG editor,

458
00:27:16.859 --> 00:27:17.859
or in C++.

459
00:27:17.859 --> 00:27:20.859
You can get information about a widget,

460
00:27:20.859 --> 00:27:23.859
about the kind of like space that it occupies on screen.

461
00:27:23.859 --> 00:27:27.859
So if you just do get cached,

462
00:27:27.859 --> 00:27:33.640
am I inside a widget? I'm not.

463
00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:35.640
Get cached geometry.

464
00:27:35.640 --> 00:27:38.640
And you can basically query a whole bunch of data about this.

465
00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:39.640
You can be like,

466
00:27:39.640 --> 00:27:41.640
where is this widget on screen?

467
00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:43.640
How much space does it occupy?

468
00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:46.640
Is this widget overlapping another widget?

469
00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:48.640
Is my mouse overlapping this widget?

470
00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:51.640
It's just worth knowing that this is here.

471
00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:53.640
It basically lets you do mathematical things

472
00:27:53.640 --> 00:27:57.640
with that widget's kind of space that it takes up on screen.

473
00:27:57.640 --> 00:27:59.640
The only thing that's worth mentioning about it

474
00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:02.640
is that it's often kind of a frame behind.

475
00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:06.640
So your cached geometry is probably going to be

476
00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:08.640
always one frame out of date.

477
00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:10.640
Unless you're doing something highly dynamic,

478
00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:11.640
most of the time that doesn't matter,

479
00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:13.640
but it's just worth mentioning.

480
00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:19.130
Yeah, I think I already kind of demonstrated this a little bit,

481
00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:22.130
but there are two kind of like ease of life,

482
00:28:22.130 --> 00:28:24.130
little shortcuts that are worth knowing about

483
00:28:24.130 --> 00:28:27.130
when you're doing stuff in the hierarchy,

484
00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:28.130
that if you have a widget

485
00:28:28.130 --> 00:28:30.130
and you kind of want to interject something

486
00:28:30.130 --> 00:28:31.130
between these two things,

487
00:28:31.130 --> 00:28:33.130
you can do wrap with,

488
00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:35.130
and that will put a widget,

489
00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:37.130
will put this widget inside another widget,

490
00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:40.130
but give that widget all of the details this had.

491
00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:44.130
So you can see this horizontal box is at position 760244

492
00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:45.130
and has these sizes.

493
00:28:45.130 --> 00:28:48.130
If I wrap this with a border,

494
00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:52.130
you can see now that border is at position 760244,

495
00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:53.130
those sizes,

496
00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:55.130
and the horizontal box is within it.

497
00:28:55.130 --> 00:28:57.130
And then if you accidentally made a mistake

498
00:28:57.130 --> 00:29:00.130
and you kind of want to remove that,

499
00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:02.130
what you can do is you can do replace with,

500
00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:04.130
and this lets you swap that widget

501
00:29:04.130 --> 00:29:05.130
for a different kind of widget,

502
00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:08.130
or quite powerfully you can say replace this with a child.

503
00:29:08.130 --> 00:29:12.130
This essentially says put this back where this thing is

504
00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:14.130
and give it all the details that it had.

505
00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:15.130
So replace with,

506
00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:17.130
and you can see we're back to the horizontal box

507
00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:21.130
having the same slot details.

508
00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:24.470
I've already talked about this a little bit,

509
00:29:24.470 --> 00:29:26.470
but just bear in mind

510
00:29:26.470 --> 00:29:28.470
that different widgets have different costs.

511
00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:31.470
There's a really good talk from Adrian Pugh

512
00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:32.470
who I'll talk about in a second

513
00:29:32.470 --> 00:29:35.470
who kind of goes into a little bit more details about this.

514
00:29:35.470 --> 00:29:40.470
There's also some good posts on what was previously Twitter

515
00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:41.470
about this kind of stuff.

516
00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:43.470
It comes up occasionally.

517
00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:46.470
Basically different widgets do cost different things.

518
00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:48.470
The kind of thing to particularly watch out for

519
00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:50.470
is overlaying widgets over the top of each other.

520
00:29:50.470 --> 00:29:54.470
So the kind of two go-to panels that people think of using

521
00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:55.470
are canvas panels and overlays.

522
00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:57.470
Both of these are quite expensive.

523
00:29:57.470 --> 00:29:59.470
Canvas panels are the worst offenders.

524
00:29:59.470 --> 00:30:01.470
And the same thing with animations.

525
00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:03.470
We've talked about the widget animation system.

526
00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:05.470
It's very powerful, gives you a lot of control,

527
00:30:05.470 --> 00:30:07.470
but it is probably the most expensive way

528
00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:08.470
to animate a widget.

529
00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:11.470
The second, the kind of more efficient way

530
00:30:11.470 --> 00:30:13.470
to animate a widget is just through Blueprint code.

531
00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:16.470
So if you can affect its position just in code

532
00:30:16.470 --> 00:30:20.470
or be that Blueprint code or C++.

533
00:30:20.470 --> 00:30:24.470
And even more efficiently is do it all through materials,

534
00:30:24.470 --> 00:30:26.470
which kind of brings us onto the retainer widget

535
00:30:26.470 --> 00:30:30.470
because that lets us utilize materials as well.

536
00:30:30.470 --> 00:30:32.470
But just to kind of quickly show this,

537
00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:39.819
images in UMG don't just have to use textures.

538
00:30:39.819 --> 00:30:41.819
Sorry if I just put an image here.

539
00:30:41.819 --> 00:30:44.819
They can also use materials.

540
00:30:44.819 --> 00:30:47.490
So you can see I've got my material options here.

541
00:30:47.490 --> 00:30:50.490
I think I made a kind of, yeah,

542
00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:52.490
I've made a material image here.

543
00:30:52.490 --> 00:30:54.490
And you can see I've just plugged in this

544
00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:55.490
some funky UI material.

545
00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:58.490
And this gives you the ability to have dynamic imagery.

546
00:30:58.490 --> 00:31:01.490
And if you do animations through your material,

547
00:31:01.490 --> 00:31:03.490
that's immensely more performant than animations

548
00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:04.490
any other way.

549
00:31:04.490 --> 00:31:05.490
So if you can do that,

550
00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:09.490
that's going to save you a lot of CPU cycles.

551
00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:13.700
Then the retainer widget,

552
00:31:13.700 --> 00:31:15.700
when we're talking about efficiency,

553
00:31:15.700 --> 00:31:17.700
the retainer widget is definitely an element

554
00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:18.700
that can come in here.

555
00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:21.700
This is going to deserve its own talk in itself.

556
00:31:21.700 --> 00:31:24.700
But just know that retainer widget is a thing.

557
00:31:24.700 --> 00:31:27.700
If I put a retainer box in my,

558
00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:29.700
I'm going to wrap this widget.

559
00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:31.700
Let's do it around our new widget

560
00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:33.700
so I'm not mucking up too much of stuff

561
00:31:33.700 --> 00:31:34.700
I've already done.

562
00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:37.700
Wrap this with a retainer box.

563
00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:48.630
So the retainer widget has a couple of different functionalities.

564
00:31:48.630 --> 00:31:50.630
One thing it lets you do

565
00:31:50.630 --> 00:31:53.630
is basically turn off rendering for the widgets below it

566
00:31:53.630 --> 00:31:55.630
so that it controls these things.

567
00:31:55.630 --> 00:31:58.630
So that your widgets aren't perpetually repositioning themselves,

568
00:31:58.630 --> 00:32:00.630
re-updating, re-visualizing.

569
00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:02.630
If you have a widget that you know will only update

570
00:32:02.630 --> 00:32:04.630
for a certain period of time

571
00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:08.630
or will snap between different states,

572
00:32:08.630 --> 00:32:10.630
you kind of don't need all of that

573
00:32:10.630 --> 00:32:13.630
laying out rendering functionality the rest of the time.

574
00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:16.630
This is a little bit like dormancy in networking.

575
00:32:16.630 --> 00:32:19.630
It's kind of the same, but for widget rendering.

576
00:32:19.630 --> 00:32:21.630
So the retainer gives us control over that thing.

577
00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:24.630
It lets us say how frequently should this thing update

578
00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:28.630
or do we even just go to more of an event-based rendering

579
00:32:28.630 --> 00:32:30.630
where I'll tell you when to update

580
00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:32.630
and we just don't update otherwise.

581
00:32:32.630 --> 00:32:35.630
But the other thing that the retainer widget lets you do

582
00:32:35.630 --> 00:32:38.630
is act as almost like a post-process.

583
00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:41.630
So if you have this effect material,

584
00:32:41.630 --> 00:32:43.630
what this will essentially do is

585
00:32:43.630 --> 00:32:45.630
if you pass in a material here,

586
00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.630
you can pass in a texture parameter name

587
00:32:47.630 --> 00:32:53.630
and the retainer widget will feed in a texture to a parameter

588
00:32:53.630 --> 00:32:55.630
with whatever name you specify here.

589
00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:57.630
And the texture that it feeds in

590
00:32:57.630 --> 00:33:01.630
will be basically a picture of all the widgets within it.

591
00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:05.630
So it gives the ability to do really cool post-process effects

592
00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:07.630
of whatever you can imagine.

593
00:33:07.630 --> 00:33:09.630
It basically takes you to take a snapshot

594
00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:11.630
of all the widgets within this retainer box

595
00:33:11.630 --> 00:33:14.630
and do screen space modifications to them.

596
00:33:14.630 --> 00:33:16.630
So you can imagine how powerful that could be.

597
00:33:16.630 --> 00:33:19.460
Just worth mentioning that it's there.

598
00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:21.460
Editor widgets.

599
00:33:21.460 --> 00:33:24.460
This is kind of straying into an entirely different topic

600
00:33:24.460 --> 00:33:27.460
of editor functionality, but just worth mentioning

601
00:33:27.460 --> 00:33:29.460
that you can create custom widgets

602
00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:32.460
for your own editor purposes.

603
00:33:32.460 --> 00:33:34.460
These are tools that you can expose to your designers,

604
00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:37.460
to whoever, and you can make widgets for them

605
00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:42.460
and kind of run editor time functions.

606
00:33:42.460 --> 00:33:45.460
You could make an editor widget for positioning all your actors

607
00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:47.460
in a nice clean row if that's something you needed

608
00:33:47.460 --> 00:33:50.460
or whatever, any kind of tool you can imagine.

609
00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:52.460
You can make an editor widget for it,

610
00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:55.460
which is a widget that has access to editor specific functionality.

611
00:33:55.460 --> 00:33:56.460
You can talk to your editor systems.

612
00:33:56.460 --> 00:33:59.460
You can edit data in your level at editor time.

613
00:33:59.460 --> 00:34:02.460
It's just worth noting that those things exist.

614
00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:04.170
UI extension points.

615
00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:07.170
When we're talking about adding editor functionality,

616
00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:10.170
one of the other things you might want to do is start,

617
00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:13.170
at some point you may want to start creating your own tools

618
00:34:13.170 --> 00:34:15.170
and one of the things you might wonder is like,

619
00:34:15.170 --> 00:34:18.170
oh, I want to add another button to this window

620
00:34:18.170 --> 00:34:21.170
or how do I just add another thing to the select section?

621
00:34:21.170 --> 00:34:25.170
Essentially, everything within Unreal

622
00:34:25.170 --> 00:34:28.170
is kind of represented by some kind of points

623
00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:30.170
that you can hook things onto.

624
00:34:30.170 --> 00:34:32.170
The way you can actually visualize these,

625
00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:34.170
they're kind of basically a bunch of secret names

626
00:34:34.170 --> 00:34:36.170
that represent all of the hook points

627
00:34:36.170 --> 00:34:37.170
you can attach these things to.

628
00:34:37.170 --> 00:34:40.170
If you go into your editor preferences

629
00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:43.170
and you go to all settings, you search for extension.

630
00:34:43.170 --> 00:34:47.320
There's this option here called display UI extension points.

631
00:34:47.320 --> 00:34:50.320
If you tick that, it'll ask you to restart the editor

632
00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:54.320
but when you do that, your editor will be blanketed

633
00:34:54.320 --> 00:34:56.320
with lots of green text everywhere

634
00:34:56.320 --> 00:35:00.320
and these will basically show you all the secret cheat code names

635
00:35:00.320 --> 00:35:02.320
for where all these hook points are

636
00:35:02.320 --> 00:35:05.320
and you can then use those when writing your,

637
00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:11.320
you'd have to have a editor module for your project.

638
00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:13.320
This would all be C++ code

639
00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:16.320
but you could then hook in your own custom windows.

640
00:35:16.320 --> 00:35:19.320
You could add buttons to custom tabs and stuff like that

641
00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:22.320
and that's all using these editor extension hook points.

642
00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:26.570
When we're talking about editor functionality

643
00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:30.570
and adding your UI, it's also worth mentioning

644
00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:32.570
a couple of extra plugins here.

645
00:35:32.570 --> 00:35:34.570
The first one is user toolbox.

646
00:35:34.570 --> 00:35:36.570
If you are thinking about doing your own custom UIs,

647
00:35:36.570 --> 00:35:41.570
it's worth mentioning that there is a kind of plugin

648
00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:43.570
available already called user toolbox

649
00:35:43.570 --> 00:35:48.570
that lets you basically just quickly add custom editor widgets

650
00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:51.570
and editor bits of functionality to a kind of toolbox

651
00:35:51.570 --> 00:35:54.570
that is easily available to all of your designers.

652
00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:56.570
This is just one of those examples of like

653
00:35:56.570 --> 00:35:59.570
you think about some kind of like cool feature or system

654
00:35:59.570 --> 00:36:01.570
and it turns out Unreal has actually already done it.

655
00:36:01.570 --> 00:36:04.570
So if you do start thinking about adding editor windows

656
00:36:04.570 --> 00:36:07.570
or editor tools that you want to position around the UI,

657
00:36:07.570 --> 00:36:09.570
do look at user toolbox first

658
00:36:09.570 --> 00:36:11.570
because you could save yourself a little bit of time there.

659
00:36:11.570 --> 00:36:16.079
When I've talked about gamepad functionality and stuff like that,

660
00:36:16.079 --> 00:36:19.079
there are a lot of problems that you commonly come up against

661
00:36:19.079 --> 00:36:21.079
whether it's like gamepad navigation,

662
00:36:21.079 --> 00:36:23.079
whether it's displaying widgets in a way

663
00:36:23.079 --> 00:36:27.079
such that you can kind of display widget A over widget B

664
00:36:27.079 --> 00:36:29.079
and then you need to be able to return to widget B

665
00:36:29.079 --> 00:36:31.079
once you close widget A.

666
00:36:31.079 --> 00:36:34.079
There are these common problems that you kind of tackle again and again and again

667
00:36:34.079 --> 00:36:37.079
when you're doing UMG stuff with Unreal.

668
00:36:37.079 --> 00:36:39.079
A lot of those problems have actually already been solved

669
00:36:39.079 --> 00:36:42.079
by an epic development plugin called Common UI.

670
00:36:42.079 --> 00:36:45.079
It's very powerful. It's very, very feature rich.

671
00:36:45.079 --> 00:36:49.079
It would be too big to kind of go into talking about right now

672
00:36:49.079 --> 00:36:52.079
but it's a plugin that's been developed and shipped with Fortnite

673
00:36:52.079 --> 00:36:55.079
so it's kind of quite thoroughly battle tested.

674
00:36:55.079 --> 00:36:57.079
Absolutely spend some time looking into it.

675
00:36:57.079 --> 00:37:00.079
The links to all three of these plugins that I'm about to talk about now

676
00:37:00.079 --> 00:37:02.079
are in this presentation by the way

677
00:37:02.079 --> 00:37:05.079
so if you kind of forget the names or whatever, the links are there.

678
00:37:05.079 --> 00:37:07.369
And the last one to mention is MVVM.

679
00:37:07.369 --> 00:37:10.369
This stands for Model View ViewModel

680
00:37:10.369 --> 00:37:12.369
which is not a real specific term.

681
00:37:12.369 --> 00:37:15.369
For anyone who's used to using kind of proprietary UI technology,

682
00:37:15.369 --> 00:37:17.369
things like Scaleform and stuff like that,

683
00:37:17.369 --> 00:37:19.369
you might be used to this system where

684
00:37:19.369 --> 00:37:22.369
your UI is kind of this independent piece of software

685
00:37:22.369 --> 00:37:26.369
that you kind of send information to and you get information back from

686
00:37:26.369 --> 00:37:30.369
and what you might have is this kind of like contractual mediator

687
00:37:30.369 --> 00:37:33.369
where there's this bit of information that sits between your game

688
00:37:33.369 --> 00:37:38.369
and the actual UI where both the UI and the game kind of agree on this thing

689
00:37:38.369 --> 00:37:40.369
and the UI doesn't actually have to know about what the game is doing

690
00:37:40.369 --> 00:37:42.369
and the game doesn't have to know about what the UI is doing.

691
00:37:42.369 --> 00:37:46.369
They both just kind of write into and read from this kind of mediated central bit of data.

692
00:37:46.369 --> 00:37:50.369
This is an implementation of really something that's called the mediator pattern.

693
00:37:50.369 --> 00:37:54.369
And that can definitely be a desirable set of functionality

694
00:37:54.369 --> 00:37:59.369
for people who just want that separation of concerns between gameplay and UI.

695
00:37:59.369 --> 00:38:01.369
These two things don't have to know about each other.

696
00:38:01.369 --> 00:38:06.369
It makes things very reusable because multiple things can use the same view model.

697
00:38:06.369 --> 00:38:09.369
It means that your UI team don't necessarily have to communicate

698
00:38:09.369 --> 00:38:11.369
quite as much with your dev team

699
00:38:11.369 --> 00:38:14.369
because your UI team can set up the view model that they need

700
00:38:14.369 --> 00:38:17.369
and then pass that over to the gameplay team

701
00:38:17.369 --> 00:38:19.369
and they can just plug the right values into that view model.

702
00:38:19.369 --> 00:38:21.369
So it lets you kind of break things up

703
00:38:21.369 --> 00:38:24.369
and it means that the teams don't necessarily have to be waiting on each other as much,

704
00:38:24.369 --> 00:38:27.369
which can be very desirable depending on your setup.

705
00:38:27.369 --> 00:38:31.719
So just know that MVVM is a plugin that's available that implements this.

706
00:38:31.719 --> 00:38:35.719
Whereas Common UI is battle-tested and shipped for Fortnite,

707
00:38:35.719 --> 00:38:37.719
MVVM is definitely experimental

708
00:38:37.719 --> 00:38:41.719
and I think the current advice is do not ship titles with this plugin,

709
00:38:41.719 --> 00:38:45.719
but by the time you've been developing a project with this for two years,

710
00:38:45.719 --> 00:38:46.719
that could change.

711
00:38:46.719 --> 00:38:48.719
So just know that it's there

712
00:38:48.719 --> 00:38:51.719
and it does provide quite a lot of useful functionality.

713
00:38:51.719 --> 00:38:56.460
The last thing is there's a couple of UMG-specific resources.

714
00:38:56.460 --> 00:39:00.460
There's a whole bunch of links actually part of this presentation,

715
00:39:00.460 --> 00:39:03.460
but just a couple of key points that I'd want to bring up.

716
00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:08.460
There's an Unreal developer who goes by the handle Ben UI.

717
00:39:08.460 --> 00:39:11.460
He's got a website dedicated to Unreal stuff,

718
00:39:11.460 --> 00:39:15.460
but specifically a lot of it is geared more to UMG stuff,

719
00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:19.460
but it is also just useful Unreal stuff content.

720
00:39:19.460 --> 00:39:21.460
He gave a talk for JetBrains

721
00:39:21.460 --> 00:39:24.460
where he basically spent a bit of time demystifying

722
00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:27.460
some of the kind of weird terminology and macros around Slate.

723
